[Music]  hello and welcome to lesson two  on scatter plots where we will be going  over the basics of plotting and  customizing scatter plots  using the built-in plotting functions so  scatter plots are probably the most  common type of plot in ecology  because it shows the relationship or  lack thereof  between two continuous variables so  first we're going to load our data and  we're just going to load the same plant  growth data as from the previous lesson  by running this line with data plant  growth it loads the built-in data  and we can see that it has two columns  which represents  plant dry weight the plants were grown  using three different treatments which  are represented by group  control treatment one and treatment two  and then they were collected and dried  and waited wait waited excuse me  i don't even know how to say that waited  and  this is in grams all right so then we're  also going to append this  extra water data that we have for the  data set which represents how much  water in liters was added to each plant  throughout its lifespan  so we're going to just run this line all  the numbers should already be in here  and then we'll add that to the plant  growth data using this dollar sign water  to create a new column  called water with these water data so as  we learned in the previous lesson  we can just simply create a plot using  the plot function  and then say weight  is a function of water and then define  the data set  data equals plant growth  and when we run that we get this very  simple  scatter plot of weight as a function of  water  now let's go over all the most common  basic customizations you will need to  know when creating figures this way  so first let's talk about the axes let's  make sure that the axis labels are more  informative  so we can do that by modifying the xlab  and y  lab arguments in the plot function so  let's just copy and paste this  so we don't have to keep rewriting it  and then  we'll hit a comma to add a new argument  and keeping in mind that r is not really  space sensitive  and so we can actually for keeping to  keep this a little bit neat instead of  having it be one long line that keeps  going out now  we can just hit return after that comma  and just add the new arguments here  just to keep it a little more organized  that's totally up to you however you  prefer to do it  all right so we'll add in the xlab  argument  and so for the x label we'll call it the  x label  is water we'll call it total water  in liters like that and another comma  there then we'll do the  y lab argument equals dried  biomass weight  in grams all right so now when we run  this  the x and the y labels have been  rewritten based on this x-lab and y-lab  argument  then let's add a little bit more space  around the points  by making the axis ranges a bit larger  using the  x-limb and the y-limb arguments so what  i mean is for example these points are  very  close to the edge and maybe just for  visual say for  composition's sake you want to add a  little more space around these points  just to keep it a little  i don't know maybe you think it'll look  better this way so  we'll do exlim equals and then you add a  little vector using the concatenate  function or the c  that ranges from the minimum x value to  the maximum x value that we want  so let's go from a little bit  more let's add a little space here by  starting at 1.25  and then go all the way to 3.75  okay and then y  limb do the same thing we'll start at  3.25 and go to  6.75 and now let's run that  and there you go added quite a bit of  space this might be too much space but  it's i'm just kind of doing this to show  to  show you how it works okay so now let's  move on to the points themselves  let's change the shape of the points to  circles that are filled in  by changing the pch parameter sometimes  open circles like this are useful  especially when you have a lot of  overlapping points because then you can  see them  but other times i think in most cases  it's nice to just have filled in circles  so we'll do that and again we'll just  copy and paste this down here  so we don't have to rewrite the whole  thing and we'll add in the arguments  or the argument pch equals  16. and if we run that you'll see that  the points are all now filled in  now you can play around there's a lot of  different pch values you know if we did  10 for example we get these circles with  crosses inside  if we did nine we get these squ  diamonds with crosses there's all sorts  of different symbols  with using pch but 16 is the most common  and the the simplest and and best to use  in most cases  so we can also change the color of the  points using the col function  and then we can even we can just use  quotes and write out  red for example and run that and now all  the points  are red it's important to keep in mind  that a good best practice  to remember is that plots are generally  much better to keep in grayscale  or black and white unless it's  absolutely necessary to include color  and in the case of this figure there  really isn't a reason to  there isn't a reason not to keep the  points black because there aren't  different categories or anything else we  want to  show it's just simply the biomass and  how it  changes with how much plants were  watered so there's really no  nothing helpful about making those  points red so  just keep it to black or we can erase  that all together since the default is  always going to be black  finally we can change the size of the  points using the cex  argument and the default is one which  represents  100 percent so the points right now are  displayed at 100 percent  of their size so if we add in cex  equals 1.5 now they will be  150 percent larger  want to make them really big we can  double their size by making cex equals  two  great so that's it for this lesson as a  quick review  we went over a review of using the plot  function  then we went over modifying the axis  labels modifying the axis ranges  modifying the shape color and size of  points  and that's it so next we're going to go  over the basics of plotting  and customizing box plots so thanks for  watching hope you enjoyed  and see you in the next lesson  [Music]

